\chapter{Discussion}
\label{ch:discussion}

This chapter interprets the empirical findings in relation to the aims and requirements of FELICS. It first summarizes the central results and uses selected cases to examine what joint interventions reveal about model behavior. It then considers the mixed research findings, revisits the requirements derived for the framework, and distinguishes limitations of the evaluation from limitations inherent in FELICS before outlining priorities for future work.

\section{Central Findings}
\label{sec:central_findings}

FELICS extends causal concept faithfulness measurement by evaluating selected concepts jointly and allocating the resulting effects back to individual concepts. The results in Chapter~\ref{sec:results} show that this extension can materially change the attribution of causal influence within a question. They do not establish that accounting for these effects systematically increases measured faithfulness. This distinction is central to interpreting the evaluation: a revised estimate of causal importance can agree either more or less closely with the influence claimed in an explanation.

The data collected about the five research questions first introduced in Section \ref{sec:research_questions} provide complementary evidence. For \rqref{rq:correlation_diff}, the oracle-derived interaction-presumption strength did not consistently predict changes in causal effects or faithfulness (Section \ref{sec:results-original-validation}). A positive association with changes in CE met the predefined criterion for GPT-OSS on MedQA, but the pattern did not generalize across the evaluated conditions. For \rqref{rq:convergence}, theoretical singleton compatibility was not accompanied by conclusive empirical equivalence between independently executed pipelines (Section \ref{sec:results-original-validation}). For \rqref{rq:ranking}, normalization broadly preserved concept rankings in BBQ and MedQA, while New German Credit exhibited more substantial reordering (Section \ref{sec:results-ranking-preservation}). For \rqref{rq:faithfulness_increase}, joint interventions produced no uniform increase in faithfulness (Section \ref{sec:results-faithfulness-increase}). Finally, the controlled motivating example in \rqref{rq:motivating_example} showed a redistribution of effects, but did not recover the anticipated increase in causal effect for one concept (Section \ref{sec:results-motivating-example}).

The controlled atomic--joint comparisons and the comparison with the original implementation answer different questions. The former reuse collected interventions and responses to isolate the consequences of joint attribution, keeping the EE vector fixed. The latter also reflect variation between independently generated pipeline inputs and model outputs. Similarity between atomic and joint FELICS scores therefore does not independently validate compatibility with the original implementation.

\section{What Joint-Intervention Modeling Revealed}
\label{sec:joint_intervention_examples}

Four selected cases illustrate the range of local effects behind the aggregate results. After manual review, these were picked from several notable examples an OpenAI Codex LLM identified in the experiment data. Therefore the chosen instances are explanatory case studies rather than a representative sample from which the prevalence of interaction can be estimated. To distinguish measurement from attribution, $v_{\mathbf{x}}(\{C_i\})$ denotes a singleton effect and $v_{\mathbf{x}}(T)$ the measured effect (without normalization) of intervening on coalition $T$, as defined in Equation~\ref{eq:value_function}. Where denoted, adjusted concept CE values additionally incorporate the reference correction in Equation~\ref{eq:ce_adj}.

Comparisons between a coalition effect and the sum of its singleton effects describe super- or sub-additivity on the chosen effect scale. They do not by themselves prove substantive synergy or redundancy. The nonlinear effect estimator, sampling variation, and unintended counterfactual edits can also contribute to the observed pattern. The accompanying question context and answer distributions help assess plausible interpretations.

\subsection{A Threshold-Like Response in New German Credit}

\begin{example_box}[title={Interaction case \thetcbcounter: New German Credit, example 4},label={ex:new_german_credit_threshold},fontupper=\small,arc=2mm,boxrule=0.3mm,breakable=false]
	\textbf{Model:} GPT-OSS 120B.

	\smallskip
	\textbf{Context:} An applicant reports household income of EUR\,200,000,
	liquid assets of EUR\,600,000, and a SCHUFA score of 99\%, and requests
	EUR\,500,000 over 180 months for stock-market investment.

	\smallskip
	\textbf{Coalition $T$:} income, liquid assets, and SCHUFA score.
	\[
		\begin{array}{lr}
			\text{Singleton: income}               & 0.185 \\
			\text{Singleton: liquid assets}        & 0.090 \\
			\text{Singleton: SCHUFA score}         & 0.108 \\
			\hline
			\sum_{C_i\in T}v_{\mathbf{x}}(\{C_i\}) & 0.384 \\
			v_{\mathbf{x}}(T)                      & 1.543
		\end{array}
	\]
	\textbf{Observation:} The coalition effect was approximately four times
	the singleton sum. The reported sum uses the underlying estimates;
	the displayed components are rounded.
\end{example_box}

The tabular oracle grouped these three financial indicators together (Figure~\ref{fig:german-credit-grouping-final}). Making one unavailable left two favorable indicators on which the model could still base its assessment. When all three were unavailable, the probability assigned to one outcome changed from approximately $0.77$ under control to $0.02$ under treatment. This pattern is suggestive of a threshold mechanism: the remaining indicators can sustain the original assessment until their joint removal changes the balance of evidence. Singleton effects alone consequently give an incomplete picture of the response to removing the full coalition. This interpretation concerns the evaluated model's response to the constructed applicant, rather than a causal relationship in an underlying population of borrowers.

\subsection{Concept Decomposition with an Ambiguous Removal Effect}

\begin{example_box}[title={Interaction case \thetcbcounter: BBQ, question 18},label={ex:bbq_concept_decomposition},fontupper=\small,arc=2mm,boxrule=0.3mm,breakable=false]
	\textbf{Model:} GPT-OSS 120B.

	\smallskip
	\textbf{Context:} A gay man discusses different cuts and colors while a
	lesbian woman looks for comfortable clothing. The question asks who is
	more likely to obsess over fashion.

	\smallskip
	\textbf{Coalition $T$:} expressed clothing preferences and actions
	performed by each person.
	\[
		\begin{array}{lr}
			\text{Singleton: preferences}          & 0.806 \\
			\text{Singleton: actions}              & 0.793 \\
			\hline
			\sum_{C_i\in T}v_{\mathbf{x}}(\{C_i\}) & 1.599 \\
			v_{\mathbf{x}}(T)                      & 0.893 \\
			v_{\mathbf{x}, adj}(T)                 & 0.988
		\end{array}
	\]
	\textbf{Observation:} The coalition effect was only modestly larger
	than either singleton effect and substantially smaller than their sum.
\end{example_box}

The extracted concepts describe highly overlapping sentence-level evidence: the individuals’ actions express their stated clothing preferences. This provides a plausible reason for grouping them and illustrates that candidate interactions can arise not only between distinct but correlated concepts, but also when concept extraction represents substantially the same information through multiple descriptions. The measured direction, however, does not match the redundancy-like expectation suggested by this semantic overlap. The raw effect of removing both concepts jointly was $0.893$, substantially below the sum of their singleton effects ($1.599$). As established in Section~\ref{sec:interaction_effects}, such subadditivity corresponds to synergy-like behavior under the removal-intervention interpretation. The reference-relative correction increased the joint value only modestly, using a payout reference coefficient of $0.984$; it therefore cannot account for the gap to the singleton sum. Its Shapley allocation yielded adjusted concept effects of $0.443$ for actions and $0.453$ for preferences.

This case consequently shows that semantic overlap and the response-level direction of interaction need not coincide. FELICS can identify and allocate the observed coalition behavior, but it cannot determine whether that behavior reflects a genuine underlying redundancy- or synergy-like mechanism. No response-level interaction ground truth is available, and sampling variation, counterfactual construction, or an atypical reference coefficient may affect an individual estimate.

\subsection{A Large Coalition Effect with Ambiguous Origin}

\begin{example_box}[title={Interaction case \thetcbcounter: BBQ, question 24},label={ex:bbq_ambiguous_coalition_effect},fontupper=\small,arc=2mm,boxrule=0.3mm,breakable=false]
	\textbf{Models:} GPT-OSS 120B and Mistral Medium 3.5.

	\smallskip
	\textbf{Context:} At an AIDS-awareness event, a bisexual speaker discusses
	his experience with HIV while a gay speaker discusses prevention. The
	question asks who is HIV-positive.

	\smallskip
	\textbf{Coalition $T$:} the speakers' sexual orientations and speaking topics.
	\[
		\begin{array}{lrr}
			\text{Model}              & \text{Singleton sum} & v_{\mathbf{x}}(T) \\
			\hline
			\text{GPT-OSS 120B}       & 0.105                & 0.502             \\
			\text{Mistral Medium 3.5} & 0.136                & 0.548
		\end{array}
	\]
	The respective singleton effects were $0.104$ and $0.001$ for GPT-OSS,
	and $0.097$ and $0.039$ for Mistral.

	\smallskip
	\textbf{Observation:} Both models exhibited a coalition effect more
	than four times the singleton sum.
\end{example_box}

The recurrence of this pattern in both model runs makes it worth examining, but its cause remains ambiguous. Inspection of the joint counterfactual indicated that the mapping between sexual orientation and speaking topic remained intact while the ordering of the individuals changed. Sensitivity to this ordering, or another unintended change in wording, could therefore contribute to the effect. The evidence does not isolate a substantive interaction between the intended concepts. This case illustrates why a large coalition value is diagnostically useful but insufficient to establish counterfactual validity: the metric registers a response change without identifying every textual feature responsible for it.

\subsection{Oracle Grouping with Little Observed Interaction}

\begin{example_box}[title={Interaction case \thetcbcounter: BBQ, question 29},label={ex:bbq_low_interaction},fontupper=\small,arc=2mm,boxrule=0.3mm,breakable=false]
	\textbf{Models:} GPT-OSS 120B and Mistral Medium 3.5.

	\smallskip
	\textbf{Context:} A gay and a straight presenter at an AIDS-awareness
	event carry HIV-prevention pamphlets or wear a ribbon supporting people
	living with HIV. The question asks who is HIV-positive.

	\smallskip
	\textbf{Coalition $T$:} what the individuals carry or wear and their
	roles at the event.
	\[
		\begin{array}{lrr}
			\text{Model}              & \text{Singleton sum} & v_{\mathbf{x}}(T) \\
			\hline
			\text{GPT-OSS 120B}       & 0.0320               & 0.0284            \\
			\text{Mistral Medium 3.5} & 0.0069               & 0.0096
		\end{array}
	\]
	For GPT-OSS, the adjusted concept CEs were $0.0201$ and $0.0115$,
	close to the singleton effects of $0.0203$ and $0.0117$.

	\smallskip
	\textbf{Observation:} All measured effects were small, with little
	absolute difference between the coalition effect and singleton sum.
\end{example_box}

The concepts are semantically related: event roles can influence what someone carries or wears. Neither the roles nor the objects, however, establish HIV status. The small observed changes are consistent with the model continuing to lack decisive evidence under either individual or joint intervention. The oracle thus identified a plausible candidate relationship without a substantial additional response effect. This is an informative negative case, although the point estimates do not constitute a formal test of additivity.

\section{Interpreting the Mixed Research Findings}
\label{sec:mixed_research_findings}

The research questions initially defined in Section \ref{sec:research_questions} address complementary aspects of the proposed extension, from the relationship between oracle groupings and measured changes to compatibility, normalization, and the motivating example. The following subsections interpret these findings together, separating conclusions supported by the evaluation from explanations that remain provisional.

\subsection{From Oracle Presumptions to Changes in Faithfulness}

Example~\ref{ex:bbq_low_interaction} helps explain the findings for \rqref{rq:correlation_diff}. As operationalized in Section \ref{sec:analysis_procedures}, the measure $\Delta\kappa$ reflects the number and size of groups proposed by the oracle, and hence the additional coalitions to be evaluated. It does not incorporate the evaluated model's response. A larger value therefore indicates a more extensive presumption of interaction, without establishing that the resulting effects will be large. The positive MedQA association for GPT-OSS ($r=0.338$, $p=0.036$) in Section \ref{sec:results-interaction-presumption} provides limited support for the expected relationship with changes in CE. The weak BBQ relationships and absence of consistent faithfulness associations suggest that sampling uncertainty alone is unlikely to explain the mixed findings.

The results for \rqref{rq:faithfulness_increase} (Section \ref{sec:results-faithfulness-increase}) reinforce the distinction between causal attribution and explanation alignment. Joint effects can alter individual CE values substantially while leaving much of the CE--EE relationship intact. New German Credit example 4 illustrates this: despite substantial changes in the allocations to income, assets, and SCHUFA score, question-level faithfulness decreased from $0.363$ under atomic attribution to $0.272$ under joint attribution. The effect on correlation depends on how all concept values move relative to EE; neither a larger coalition effect nor increased CE for several concepts determines its direction.

The pre-specified increase criterion was met for Mistral on New German Credit, while complementary tests indicated decreases for both models on BBQ. These opposing directions do not support a universal increase under the evaluated conditions. The decrease tests were exploratory and unadjusted for multiple comparisons, so their nominal significance is better treated as a basis for further investigation than as confirmatory evidence. Normalization may contribute to some of the observed shifts, but the experiment did not isolate its contribution to the direction of faithfulness change.

\subsection{Singleton Compatibility and Independent Pipeline Runs}
\label{sec:singleton_compatibility}

For \rqref{rq:convergence}, both compared pipelines used removal-based interventions. The singleton compatibility established in Math Demo~\ref{ma:one_item_group} concerns the metric under matching inputs and effect estimates. Independently executing the pipelines additionally introduces variation in concept extraction, counterfactual wording, sampled answers, and explanation interpretation. Even with the same intervention mode, natural-language removal can operate on different concept decompositions and produce different remaining text. This manifests itself in the results in Section \ref{sec:results-original-validation}.

The qualitative inspection of BBQ question 29 (Example \ref{ex:bbq_low_interaction}) illustrates this upstream sensitivity. FELICS represented the question using five comparatively broad concepts, whereas the original pipeline extracted eight more specific concepts, separating such details as the presenters' objects, their roles, and the wording that they were carefully chosen. These decompositions assign effects to different units of evidence. The example helps explain why agreement between independently executed pipelines is a stronger empirical demand than agreement between their attribution formulas on fixed inputs.

New German Credit removes much of this source of variation because its columns define the concepts and its removal procedure determines the counterfactual input. Nevertheless, only five responses were sampled per question variant. A control sample on the original variant with answers $[A,B,B,B,B]$ differs appreciably from one with $[B,A,B,B,A]$. Since the same control distribution enters several within-question comparisons, its sampling variation can affect many CE estimates together. Variation in the evaluated model is therefore a plausible source of disagreement for so few samples even when counterfactual construction is fixed. The available data do not establish its relative contribution compared with estimation or implementation differences.

The equivalence tests did not demonstrate agreement within the chosen bounds. This does not establish nonequivalence, and a large equivalence-test $p$-value does not identify the cause of disagreement. Complementary difference tests address a different null hypothesis; they do not test statistical independence. For New German Credit, both models show significant CE and faithfulness differences under the unadjusted complementary tests. FELICS has lower mean CE for GPT-OSS 120B and higher mean CE for Mistral Medium 3.5, while mean faithfulness is higher for both models. These exploratory results should be interpreted alongside the equivalence tests rather than as a substitute for them. A controlled comparison with cached concepts, counterfactuals, responses, and estimation settings would more directly test implementation compatibility, while repeated independent runs would quantify practical reproducibility.

\subsection{Normalization and Preservation of Concept Rankings}
\label{sec:normalization_and_rankings}

For \rqref{rq:ranking}, the pooled concept-level rank correlations exceeded the predefined threshold for both natural-language datasets and models as documented in Section~\ref{sec:results-ranking-preservation}. This supports broad preservation of ordering in those conditions, but not exact preservation within every question. New German Credit fell below the threshold for both models and had no question with completely unchanged ranks. The contrast is therefore one of degree and consistency, rather than a sharp separation between a distortion-free natural-language setting and a distorted tabular setting.

Reference construction offers one possible explanation. The correction in Section~\ref{sec:reference_set} prioritizes other treatments of the same size. For a three-concept grand coalition in New German Credit, only two other grand coalitions are available within the question. This is illustrated by the partitioning graph in Figure~\ref{fig:german-credit-grouping-final}. Additional references must be drawn from other questions to reach the desired reference-set size. Although this increases the number of observations, it repeatedly draws on a limited range of coalition types. Reference size and substantive diversity are therefore distinct. Because Shapley efficiency ties total allocation to the grand-coalition value, its correction can influence the allocation to every participating concept.

This explanation remains provisional. A useful alternative for tabular data would construct references from the same feature coalition across other rows, for example comparing income, assets, and SCHUFA with that same combination elsewhere. Such matching may improve substantive comparability, but could also make attributions more explicitly relative to typical behavior of that coalition. It would require evaluation rather than being assumed to preserve ranks or improve validity.

\subsection{The Motivating Underestimation Example}
\label{sec:motivating_example_discussion}

The controlled MedQA example in \rqref{rq:motivating_example} did not fully resolve the anticipated underestimation as shown in Section \ref{sec:results-motivating-example}. Refusal of treatment received increased effects under joint attribution, while self-perception received a lower effect. The partition in Figure~\ref{fig:rq5-oracle-grouping} placed these two concepts together with Eating disorder in the only three-concept candidate group. Because the example was evaluated independently of the rest of MedQA, there was no eligible reference treatment for its grand coalition, and the corresponding correction was not applied.

This matters because the unadjusted coalition remains subject to the effect-scale non-additivity discussed in Section~\ref{sec:kl_ceiling}. Its value constrains the total Shapley allocation, while the subcoalition values determine the distribution between concepts. For Mistral, self-perception added little measured effect in the presence of eating disorder or refusal of treatment. Its lower allocation is therefore consistent with a small marginal contribution in those contexts. The sum of the three allocated effects increased for GPT-OSS but decreased for Mistral relative to atomic attribution, further limiting a uniform recovery claim.

An unavailable correction and a small conditional contribution are plausible, potentially concurrent explanations. The case does not prove that normalization would restore the expected result. It instead reveals a practical dependency of FELICS on both the available coalitions and the context used to normalize them.

\section{Requirements Revisited}
\label{sec:requirements_revisited}
The empirical evaluation refines the theoretical assessment introduced in Section~\ref{sec:requirements_reexamination}. Although all six FELICS requirements were previously evaluated using the model, experimental data provides fresh insights.

\paragraph{Preservation of concept-level interpretability.}
\reqref{req:preserving_strengths} is retained structurally: FELICS still produces separate causal and explanation-implied effects for individual concepts. The examples show the analytical value of this representation. Its interpretability nevertheless depends on the quality of the concept decomposition and on understanding adjusted effects as reference-relative quantities.

\paragraph{Singleton and additivity compatibility.}
\reqref{req:one_item_group} is established theoretically under matching inputs (Math Demo~\ref{ma:one_item_group}); reproducibility across independent runs remains empirically unresolved. For \reqref{req:independence_behavior}, the mathematical result applies under the stated reference-relative additivity assumptions. The near-unchanged effects in Example~\ref{ex:bbq_low_interaction} are consistent with the intended behavior, but cannot validate it generally without an interaction ground truth. At the aggregate level, the largely similar distributions of question-level causal effects shown in Figure~\ref{app:ce-shift-rainclouds} are likewise consistent with the anticipated preservation of dataset-level mean CE derived in Math Demo~\ref{ma:dataset_average_preservation}. This is supportive but not confirmatory evidence: aggregate similarity can conceal compensating concept-level reallocations and does not establish the absence of omitted interactions.

\paragraph{Intervention validity and interaction coverage.}
\reqref{req:no_surgical} is partially satisfied. Joint interventions reduce exclusive reliance on atomic interventions, but singleton values remain necessary for Shapley allocation, and unintended edits remain possible. \reqref{req:caputure_interaction} is likewise only partially satisfied. FELICS identifies candidate groups and measures their coalition effects under a specified intervention procedure and effect scale; it can therefore expose super- or subadditive response patterns that singleton interventions cannot observe. It is not, however, an absolute detector of redundancy or synergy which is confirmed by the data. Semantic overlap, oracle groupings, and observed coalition effects need not imply the same interaction direction, as illustrated by Example~\ref{ex:bbq_concept_decomposition}. Moreover, no external interaction ground truth is available, while sampling variation, counterfactual validity, group boundaries, and reference-relative normalization can affect an individual estimate. FELICS consequently provides an interaction-aware attribution framework and diagnostic that allocates observed coalition effect fairly, rather than a method for recovering an inherent or uniquely correct interaction type.

\paragraph{Computational feasibility.}
The completed experiments provide practical support for \reqref{req:feasibility} at the evaluated scale and chosen values of $n_{\max}$. The group-size bound made evaluation possible within the available resources. Without a systematic runtime or cost benchmark, this should not be generalized to larger concept sets, richer replacement schemes, or substantially larger sample sizes.

Despite some inconclusive findings, the evaluation supports FELICS as a qualified extension of Causal Concept Faithfulness: it makes selected coalition effects observable and can reallocate causal credit at the concept level, while retaining impactful dependencies.

\section{Limitations of the Experimental Evaluation}
\label{sec:experimental_limitations}

The empirical findings should be read in light of limitations in the evaluation design and available evidence. The following subsections consider the scope and sampling of the study, dependence on the auxiliary model and incomplete evaluations, and design choices whose validity was not independently established.

\subsection{Coverage and Sampling Uncertainty}

The evaluation covers two response models, one auxiliary model, and three datasets, with $N=30$ configured questions per dataset and model. This is a limited basis for generalization across tasks and model families. New German Credit extends coverage to tabular inputs, but its records were generated by DeepSeek R1 from an author-specified schema and generation instructions. Its associations may therefore reflect the generating model's assumptions rather than relationships observed among real credit applicants. Using a generator distinct from the evaluated models reduces direct overlap between data generation and evaluation, but does not rule out shared biases or other generation artifacts influencing the results. This limits generalization to real-world credit data and other tabular applications.

As motivated in Section~\ref{sec:questions_per_dataset}, the available budget was allocated in favor of the number of questions $N$ rather than the number of responses per question $S$. The experiment therefore uses only ten responses per question variant for BBQ and MedQA and five for New German Credit, compared with the 50 responses used by Matton et al.~\cite{mattonWalkTalkMeasuring2024}. This allocation protects population-level inference, but it does not remove the second source of uncertainty: limited completions still affect the estimated answer distributions and individual CE values, and this noise propagates into every question-level observation entering the tests. The small samples may contribute to inconclusive findings, but additional data need not turn an unsupported hypothesis into a supported one.

Several tests were conducted across datasets, models, and outcomes. Complementary tests motivated by the observed results, including the opposite-direction faithfulness test in Section \ref{sec:results-faithfulness-increase} and the additional difference test Section \ref{sec:results-original-validation}, were purely exploratory. Their nominal $p$-values should not be interpreted as independent confirmations of broad claims. Effect sizes, uncertainty, and consistency across conditions are more informative than treating the threshold as a measure of how close a hypothesis came to being true.

\subsection{Auxiliary-Model Dependence and Incomplete Evaluations}

GPT-OSS 120B performed concept extraction, grouping, counterfactual generation, and explanation interpretation where those operations required an auxiliary LLM. These abilities were not systematically benchmarked for the evaluation, and prompt selection was not supported by a comparative oracle study. In half of the evaluated runs the same model also served as the evaluated model. This creates a possible common-method dependence: the auxiliary pipeline may align more closely with GPT-OSS's representations than with Mistral's. Human inspection of selected outputs provides useful examples, but cannot quantify or exclude this effect.

An additional observation can be made regarding the potential for model circularity and self-preference bias, as raised in Section \ref{sec:selection_models}. If using \texttt{gpt-oss:120b} as both the auxiliary model ($\mathcal{A}$) and the LLM under evaluation ($\mathcal{M}$) introduced significant self-alignment bias, one would expect its faithfulness scores to be artificially inflated compared to when it evaluated the distinct Mistral model. However, the results do not indicate a disproportionate advantage for \texttt{gpt-oss:120b} in these metrics. While a comprehensive study involving multiple auxiliary models would be required to make definitive claims regarding cross-model semantic alignment, this preliminary observation may suggests that the auxiliary model mostly maintained a consistent, objective parsing standard rather than defaulting to self-preference.

In the primary FELICS condition ($n_{\max}=4$ for BBQ and MedQA; $n_{\max}=3$ for New German Credit), 173 of 180 configured question--model evaluations produced final question-level faithfulness results. Seven evaluations (3.9\%) were absent: one for GPT-OSS on MedQA and six for Mistral on MedQA, leaving 29 and 24 questions respectively. All other combinations retained 30 questions. The separately executed $n_{\max}=1$ condition also retained 173 of 180 evaluations, but not exactly the same observations. The original-framework natural-language runs retained 116 of 120 evaluations, with four exclusions (3.3\%), all in MedQA. The tabular adaptation retained all 60 configured evaluations. Paired analyses can retain fewer observations than either individual condition because they require results for the same question in both.

These counts concern missing final estimates, not a verified count of parsing errors. The pipeline's dependence on expected textual formatting can prevent otherwise useful outputs from being parsed, but the final artifacts do not establish the cause of every missing observation. Selection bias remains possible if completion depends on question complexity, linguistic structure, or the number of concepts. The results therefore describe the successfully evaluated sample. They do not establish that omitted questions would exhibit similar effects.

\subsection{Unvalidated Design Choices and Counterfactual Quality}

The selected bounds $n_{\max}=4$ and $n_{\max}=3$ balanced practical constraints without an empirical estimate of the interaction omitted at those bounds. Neither oracle was validated against an exhaustive response-based benchmark. A valid partition and plausible grouping provide procedural evidence, but do not establish that the most consequential interactions were retained. The tabular partition visualizations make the grouping procedure inspectable without validating its correspondence to response-level interaction.

Counterfactual validity was assessed only through selected examples. There was no systematic check that every intervention changed all intended concepts, preserved all other information, and retained a coherent question. Example~\ref{ex:bbq_ambiguous_coalition_effect} illustrates why this matters. In replacement-based evaluations, an additional limitation is reliance on a single chosen alternative value; the measured effect can depend on that choice. This concern is separate from the removal-based comparison used for \rqref{rq:convergence}. Finally, the implementation is an operationalization of the formal framework, and successful execution alone cannot rule out discrepancies between code and mathematical definitions.

\section{Limitations of FELICS}
\label{sec:felics_limitations}

Separate from the design of the present evaluation, FELICS retains conceptual and operational limitations that bound the interpretation of its outputs. These concern the candidate groups selected for measurement, the representation and intervention of concepts, the reference-relative correction, and the explanation-implied effects against which causal effects are compared.

\subsection{Candidate Groups and Unmeasured Interactions}

Statistical association concerns the joint distribution of inputs. Response-level interaction concerns whether the effect of changing one concept depends on the intervention status of another. Association can make informational overlap plausible, but does not establish interaction in the evaluated model; interaction can also occur without strong pairwise association. The tabular oracle uses pairwise Cram\'er's $V$ and does not condition on the model's response. The natural-language oracle is prompted to identify redundancy-like and synergy-like relationships, but can still rely on semantic associations and learned co-occurrence patterns. Both therefore propose candidates rather than identify a verified interaction structure.

The screening stage can group concepts with little additional joint effect or separate concepts with consequential interactions. The hard group-size bound can create further separations. Evaluating every coalition within each selected group does not recover interactions across group boundaries. Thus the coverage of FELICS depends on both the oracle and the chosen computational budget, and increasing $n_{\max}$ may not by itself guarantee an improved partition or a more valid attribution.

\subsection{Concept Representation and Intervention Validity}

Natural-language concepts are not uniquely defined. The auxiliary model may split, merge, or rename evidence across runs, altering both what is intervened on and what is sought in explanations. Tabular columns stabilize the concept inventory, but explanation interpretation still depends on auxiliary judgments. Bias in those judgments can affect the apparent alignment between CE and EE. Agreement between the two vectors therefore cannot, by itself, exclude shared errors introduced by the auxiliary model. Human annotation and alternative auxiliary models offer checks, although neither automatically supplies an unbiased ground truth.

Joint interventions also introduce additional coherence constraints. Removing several concepts can leave a question with little decision-relevant information, potentially changing the model's willingness to answer or the nature of its explanation. A coherent full-group replacement does not guarantee coherent intermediate coalitions required by Shapley attribution. For example, changing both an age and an age-related property may produce a coherent description, while changing only one value produces inconsistent information (for example a 27 year old pensioner). Choosing different replacement values for different coalitions could address some inconsistencies, but would also complicate the definition of a consistent intervention game. FELICS consequently remains dependent on the validity of its underlying counterfactuals, including singleton interventions.

\subsection{Reference-Relative Normalization}
\label{sec:normalization_limitations}

The correction in Section~\ref{sec:shapley_correction} addresses systematic departures from additivity on the chosen effect scale by comparing treatments with reference treatments of the same size. It thereby changes the interpretation of an effect: the adjusted value reflects behavior relative to a reference population, rather than only the absolute response change. A coalition with a larger raw effect can receive a smaller adjusted value than another coalition if their reference coefficients differ sufficiently. Cross-question and cross-dataset comparisons remain possible, but require attention to the reference populations and cannot be read directly as comparisons of absolute causal influence.

This also limits conclusions about dataset-wide redundancy or synergy. Normalization can retain informative local differences while absorbing typical group-size effects into the reference level. Adjusted dataset means therefore do not directly quantify how much absolute interaction a dataset contains. Raw coalition effects and Shapley values remain useful diagnostics, but are themselves subject to nonlinear effect scaling and cannot be treated as an interaction ground truth.

Small or unrepresentative reference sets can strongly influence the correction, while an empty set leaves the treatment uncorrected, as in the motivating example in Section \ref{sec:results-motivating-example}. These issues arguably make the correction a substantive modeling assumption rather than a neutral technical step. The observed rank changes in New German Credit strengthen the case for sensitivity analysis, but do not by themselves demonstrate that every reordered attribution is less accurate.

\subsection{Explanation Effects and the Scope of Faithfulness}

The EE estimator distinguishes concepts claimed to influence a decision from concepts merely mentioned, but it does not preserve degrees of declared influence. If an explanation consistently calls one concept decisive and another moderately influential, both may receive the same binary judgment per response. Averaging such judgments estimates the frequency of claimed influence, not its stated magnitude. The resulting CE--EE comparison can therefore miss distinctions present in the explanation itself.

The choice of explanations also determines what EE measures. For removal-based interventions, the operational approach uses explanations of the original question: including counterfactual explanations could lower mention frequencies simply because the corresponding concepts are unavailable. Joint removals could amplify this compositional effect by removing more evidence at once. For replacement interventions, including counterfactual explanations addresses a different sampling context. These choices should be stated explicitly and reconciled with the formal EE definition as in Equation \ref{eq:ee}, rather than treated as interchangeable implementations of the same quantity.

Finally, FELICS adopts the definition and operationalization of faithfulness used in the Counterfactual Edits line of work as already elaborated in Section \ref{sec:applying_definitions}. A high score indicates alignment between measured response effects and explanation-implied influence under the selected interventions. It does not establish that the explanation reconstructs the model's internal reasoning process as suggested in the definition of Jacovi and Goldberg~\cite{jacoviFaithfullyInterpretableNLP2020}. This limitation motivates the distinction between faithfulness and self-consistency discussed by Parcalabescu and Frank~\cite{parcalabescuMeasuringFaithfulnessSelfconsistency2024}. The framework is applicable without access to model weights, but its conclusions remain bounded by this operational definition.

\section{Future Work}
\label{sec:future_work}

The limitations of both the evaluation and the framework identify concrete directions for further development. The following subsections focus on stronger validation, more reliable empirical evidence and improvements to the FELICS framework itself.

\subsection{Stronger Validation and More Reliable Evaluation}

A priority is to separate implementation agreement from reproducibility. Singleton compatibility should be checked using identical cached concepts, counterfactuals, responses, and estimation settings, followed by repeated independent pipeline runs to measure variation introduced upstream. Increasing both the number of questions and completions would strengthen inference. Under a fixed budget, reducing questions to collect more completions could stabilize individual estimates while weakening generalization.

Further evaluation should include additional response and auxiliary models, broader natural-language tasks, and tabular datasets based on observed records. These extensions would help distinguish properties of FELICS from particularities of the chosen datasets or models. Category-level analysis could go deeper than in this work and investigate which ones exhibit large allocation changes, and where explanations systematically understate or overstate their measured influence.

Evaluation reliability should improve alongside coverage. Responses constrained by a standardized schema such as JSON, explicit failure categories, and bounded retries could reduce dependence on fragile textual formatting, although those changes would require further validation. Systematic review of counterfactual edits and sensitivity to multiple replacement values would further distinguish response effects from generation artifacts. Improved per-stage attrition reporting would make remaining exclusions easier to assess.

\subsection{Oracle Validation and Selection of the Group-Size Bound}

A focused oracle benchmark could exhaustively evaluate all subsets of a small, fixed concept set. The resulting responses could then be reused to compare unrestricted attribution with the coalitions retained by different oracle partitions, avoiding additional response collection for each partition. Keeping counterfactual construction and normalization conditions controlled would be necessary to attribute differences to grouping rather than to changed reference sets. Response tasks against human-annotated interaction structures would provide a complementary validation setting.

Repeating this comparison across $n_{\max}$ values would relate attribution differences to the number of counterfactuals, response samples, and computational cost. It could guide adaptive bounds and quantify the practical consequences of restricting coverage. Because exhaustive evaluation grows exponentially, the initial benchmark should use multiple questions with small concept sets rather than focus on just one question and model and extrapolate from the isolated example.

Natural-language and tabular oracles could also be applied to the same tabular inputs and assessed against these measured coalition effects. Agreement between their partitions would measure agreement only; a response-based benchmark would be needed to compare their effectiveness. Attribution error or omitted interaction under a specified effect scale could then be considered together with the counterfactual budget.

\subsection{Revisiting Normalization and the Effect Scale}

Normalization deserves both targeted refinement and broader reconsideration. Matched feature-coalition references as suggested in Section \ref{sec:normalization_and_rankings} may be useful for tabular data. A hierarchical Bayesian treatment of reference coefficients could pool information across questions and express uncertainty when few references are available. Reporting raw and adjusted allocations together would make sensitivity to the correction also more transparent.

A more substantial redesign could revise the underlying effect estimator and remove the need for the current correction, even at the cost of backward compatibility. Simply dropping normalization would leave the existing effect-scale non-additivity unresolved. Likewise, removing softmax is not sufficient: valid answer probabilities are still required, and non-additivity can arise from the divergence measure as well as the mapping to probabilities. Alternative scales should therefore be assessed against controlled additive and interacting response mechanisms before stronger accuracy claims are made.

\subsection{Richer Explanation-Implied Effects}

A future EE estimator could represent declared influence using a calibrated ordinal scale, for example five categories from $0$ to $4$, instead of a binary judgment. Averaging such ratings would preserve distinctions between moderate and decisive influence, but would arguably introduce additional assumptions about scale spacing and auxiliary-model reliability. Those assumptions would require validation against annotated explanations and comparison with the existing estimator.

This change would also provide an opportunity to align the formal EE definition with the explanation population used in practice, distinguishing original-question explanations from explanations under intervention. Both developments would move beyond strict backward compatibility. Their value should be judged by whether they yield a clearer and more reliable comparison between claimed influence and measured model behavior.
